---
import PageLayout from "../../layouts/default-no-header.astro";
const title =
	"Story walk: an immersive game designed to encourage students and staff at Halmstad University to go outdoors. - Portfolio of Manaj Chakrobarti";
const bodyClass = "works";
const fontURL =
	"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Antic+Didone&family=Questrial&display=swap";
import Footer from "../../layouts/components/footer.astro";
import "../../styles/prototyping-experiences.scss";
---

<PageLayout title={title} bodyClass={bodyClass} fontURL={fontURL}>
	<main class="workspage">
		<div class="page">
			<header class="header">
				<section class="inside">
					<h1>
						Story walk: an immersive game designed to encourage
						students and staff at Halmstad University to go
						outdoors.
					</h1>

					<div class="container">
						<div class="heroimages">
							<figure>
								<img
									src="/images/works/mobile-game/hero1.png"
									alt="Story Walk game preview, tapping station"
								/>
								<figcaption>
									<span
										>Story Walk game preview, tapping
										station
									</span>
								</figcaption>
							</figure>
						</div>

						<h2 class="subheading">
							We designed a playful mobile game concept for
							students and staff to enjoy outside. Each game has 4
							missions that take 7 to 10 minutes to complete. A
							mission includes an audio narration of the
							objective, turn-by-turn voice-guided walking
							directions, and, upon completion, a congratulatory
							message for the player. The mission completes when
							the player taps their phone in an NFC tag installed
							in a station. Upon completing all the missions, they
							are offered a physical reward.
						</h2>
					</div>
				</section>
			</header>

			<article>
				<nav class="article-navigation">
					<ul class="no-list">
						<li class="first"><a href="#">Back to top</a></li>
						<li><a href="#introduction">Introduction</a></li>
						<li>
							<a href="#prototype">Prototyping</a>
						</li>
						<li>
							<a href="#ia">Information Architecture</a>
						</li>
						<li><a href="#process">Design Process</a></li>
						<li><a href="#solution">Final Prototype</a></li>
						<li><a href="#learning">Learning</a></li>
						<li class="last">
							<a href="#references">References</a>
						</li>
					</ul>
				</nav>

				<section class="section-container" id="ia">
					<h3>Information Architecture</h3>

					<div class="prototyping-body">
						<figure class="introduction-image">
							<img
								src="/images/works/mobile-game/mobile-screens.png"
								alt="High-fidelity wireframe screens from the mobile game
"
							/>
							<figcaption>
								<span
									>High-fidelity wireframe screens from the
									mobile game
								</span>
							</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<div class="section-body">
							<p>
								The system we designed has two parts. The first
								part is the mobile app, which involves listing
								stories; each story has its own home screen. We
								kept it small with only an action button to
								start the game. However, to pause or go back, we
								have a dedicated bar in the same place on all
								screens.
							</p>

							<p>
								The second part is stations around the campus;
								we kept them simple and recognizable, with a
								signboard and branding that match the app. We
								kept the messaging simple and included a URL, so
								if we change something in the app, we wouldn’t
								need to update the signboards; we would only
								update the link page.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="section-container" id="process">
					<h3>Design Process</h3>
					<figure class="introduction-image">
						<img
							src="/images/works/mobile-game/drawing.png"
							alt="Low-fidelity drawing of the story game
"
						/>
						<figcaption>
							<span>Low-fidelity drawing of the story game </span>
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<div class="section-body">
						<p>
							Our mission revolved around walking and storytelling
							from the early stage of the ideation process. We
							often sketched to understand the game's flow. Made
							prototypes to visualize objects to clear
							understanding. Once our first mobile wireframes were
							ready, we started working on the game's details.
						</p>
						<p>
							We wrote some stories for our earlier version of the
							game, but the mobile version also required narration
							for each mission. We wrote the texts, the game
							narration, and the turn-by-turn voice navigation.
							For faster prototyping, we used Amazon Polly and
							ElevenLabs to generate text-to-speech. Later, we
							used Mixpad and GarageBand to add sound effects to
							create the illusion of our story's timeline, which
							is set in dystopian Sweden. We collected all the
							required sounds from Pixabay.com.
						</p>
					</div>

					<figure class="introduction-image">
						<img
							src="/images/works/mobile-game/blendar.png"
							alt="Prototyping in Blender
"
						/>
						<figcaption>
							<span>Prototyping in Blender </span>
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<div class="section-body">
						<p>
							We used Figma to prototype, but it lacked audio
							support, so we had to convert the audio to video in
							iMovie with a black screen. Then we used the video
							files at a very low resolution to play audio.
						</p>
						<p>
							For map information, since it's on the university
							campus, all the paths and hallways weren't part of
							Google Maps or OpenStreetMap. But then we realize we
							already use Mazemap on our campus, which also has an
							API. We decided to use it, but for the prototype, we
							created SVG maps of the campus paths.
						</p>
						<p>
							We tested with some students from the studio, but
							due to different walking speeds, our audio didn’t
							match during turn-by-turn directions. We fixed it
							after several rounds of trial and error, at an
							average speed.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="section-container" id="solution">
					<h3>Final Prototype</h3>
					<figure class="introduction-image">
						<img
							src="/images/works/mobile-game/final.png"
							alt="Some screens from the final prototype"
						/>
						<figcaption>
							<span>Some screens from the final prototype </span>
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<div class="section-body">
						<p>
							The final prototype consisted of a single game,
							narrated from start to finish. Each game consists of
							4 missions, which are chosen at random from 10
							points around the campus. If any person is near any
							station, that station would be avoided. So if the
							player is playing for the second time or when 10
							players start playing, not everyone will get the
							same missions.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="section-container" id="learning">
					<h3>Learning</h3>

					<div class="section-body collumn">
						<p>
							As a digital designer, I had worked with various
							prototype dimensions, but all were for screens. In
							this project, I worked with physical materials,
							including cardboard, 3D printers, a UV printing
							machine, and laser cutters. For 3D printers, I had
							to learn 3D software like Blender, and for sound
							effects, I had to learn sound editing and mixing.
							These learnings made me a better designer by
							teaching me to shape myself to project needs.
						</p>

						<p>
							Also, for this project, we participated in two
							design sprints and one design residence session.
							Both helped me overcome my procrastination by
							teaching me to start designing with some
							restrictions, such as a time limit.
						</p>

						<p>
							However, with high learning opportunities, there
							were some areas where I could have improved. One
							such thing is keeping the stations necessary for
							taping the phone. It breaks the immersive flow and
							burden player with cognitive load. Instead, we
							could’ve used other technology, e.g., GPS, to track
							mission completion.
						</p>

						<p>
							Finally, the game was simple enough to make students
							and staff walk to a known place with a different
							identity in each story, which made it fun and a
							topic to discuss among friends, and these walks
							helped them mentally. Also, with little change to
							the system, we could create an accessible version of
							the game.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="section-container" id="references">
					<h3>References</h3>
					<div class="section-body fullsize">
						<ol>
							<li>
								ASHLEY A V BURDICK, SARAH M CAMHI (2024). The
								Effects of a Guided Mindful Walk on Mental
								Health in University Students
							</li>
							<li>
								Koorosh Aghabozorgi, Alexander van der Jagt,
								Simon Bell, Harry Smith (2025). The role of
								university campus landscape characteristics in
								students’ mental health
							</li>
							<li>
								Lim, Y.K., Stolterman, E., and Tenenberg, J.
								(2008). The anatomy of prototypes: Prototypes as
								filters, prototypes as manifestations of design
								ideas.
							</li>
						</ol>
					</div>
				</section>
			</article>
		</div>
		<Footer />
	</main>
</PageLayout>
